    Section 9 Database and Analytics

Database Introduction

    Database Intro
       Storing data on disk (EFS, EBS, EC2 Instance Store, S3) can have its limits 
       Sometimes you want to store data in a database
       You can structure the data
       You build indexed to efficiently query / search through the data
       You define relationships between your datasets

       Databases are optimized for a purpose and come with different features, shapes 
       and constraints
    
    Relational Databases
        Looks like Excel spreadsheets, with links between them!
        Can use SQL language to perform queries / lookups

    NoSQL Database
        NoSQL = non-SQL = non relational database
        Benefits:
            Flexible: easy to evolve data model
            Scalability: designed to scale-out by using distributed clusters
            High performance: optimized for a specific data model
            Highly functional: types optimized for the data model
        Ex: Key-value, document, graph, in-memory, search databases
    
    NoSQL data example: JSON
        JSON = JavaScript Object Notation
        JSON is a common form of data that fits into a NoSQL model
        Data can be nested
        Fields can change over time
        Support for new types: arrays, etc..

    Databases and Shared Responsibility on AWS
        AWS offers use to manage different databases
        Benefits include:
            Quick provisioning, high avail, vert and horz scaling
            Automated backup and restore, operations, upgrades
            OS patching is handled by AWS
            Monitoring alerting
        Note: many databases technologies could be run on EC2, but you must handle yourself the resiliency, backup, patching, high avail, fault tol, scaling

RDS & Aurora Overview
    RDS Overview
        RDS stands for Relational Database Service
        Its a managed DB service for DB use SQL as a query language
        It allows you to create databases in the cloud that are managed by AWS
            Postges
            MySQL
            MariaDB
            Oracle
            Microsoft SQL Server
            IBM DB2
            Aurora (AWS Proprietary database)

    Advantages over using RDS vs deploying DB on EC2
        RDS is a managed service
            Automated provisioning OS patching
            Continuous backups restore
            Monitoring Dashboards
            Read replicas
            Multi AZ setup for Disaster Recovery
            Maint windows 
            Scaling capability (vert and horiz)
            Storage backed by EBS
        But you can SSH into your instances
    
    RDS Solution Architecture
                          ---->   [EC2 Instance]   <----->
        [Load Balancer]   ---->   [EC2 Instance]   <-----> [Amazon RDS]
                          ---->   [EC2 Instance]   <----->
        
    Amazon Aurora
        Aurora is a proprietary technology from AWS (not open source)
        PostfreSQL and MySQL are both supported as Aurora DB
        Aurora is AWS cloud optimized and cloaims 5x performance over MySQL on RDS, over 3x the preformance of Postgres on RDS
        Aurora storage automatically grows in increments of 10GB up to 256TB
        Aurora costs more than RDS (20% more) but is more efficient
    
    Amazon Aurora Serverless
        Automated database instantiation and auto-scaling based on actual usage
        PostgresSQL and MySQL are both supported as Aurora Serverless DB
        No capacity planning needed
        Least management overhead
        Pay per second can be more cost-effective
        Use cases: good for infrequennt intermittent or unpredictable workloads

        Client connects to a proxy fleet (managed by Aurora) will scale up and down services
    
RDS Hands On
    Go to Aurora and RDS service 


RDS Deployments Options

    RDS Deployments: Read Replicas, Multi AZ
        Read Replicas
            Scale the read workload of your DB 
            Can create up to 15 read replicas
            Data is only written to the main DB
        Multi-AZ
            Failover in case of AZ outage (high availability)
            Data is only read/written to the main db
            Can only have 1 other AZ as failover 
    
    Multi Region
        Multi-Region (read replicas)
            Disaster recovery in case of region issue
            Local performance for global reads
            Replication cost

            How it works:
                In a single region we have main Amazon RDS (eu-west-1)
                We have replicas in other regions (eu-east-1)
                All writes must happen to the main RDS but reads can happen in same region so fast reads


ElastiCache Overview
    Elasticache Overview
        The same way RDS is to get managed Relational Databases
        ElastiCache is to get managed Redis or Memchached
        Caches are in-memory databases with high performance low latency
        Helps reduce load off databases for read intensive workloads

        AWS takes care of OS maint / patching, optimizations, setup, configuration, monitoring, failure recovery and backups
    
    ElastiCache solution architecture - cache
                                                             <---->  [ElastiCache In-memory DB]
        ----> [Elastic Load Balancer]  ---> [EC2 Instance] 
                                                             <---->  [Amazon RDS SQL DB]

DynamoDB Overview 

    DynamoDB
        Fully managed highly available with replication across 3 AZ
        NoSQL database not a relational database
        Scales to massive workloads distributed serverless database
        Millions of request per seconds trillions of row 100sTBs
        Fast and consistent in performance
        Single digit ms latency - low latency retrieval
        Integrated with IAM for security auth and admin
        Low cost and auto scaling capabilities
        Standard & Infrequent access

    DynamoDB - Type of data
        Is a key / value database

    DynamoDB Accelerator - DAX
        Fully managed in-memory cache for DynamoDB
        10x performance improvement
        Secure highly scalable a HA
        ElastiCache can be used for other DB while DAX can only be used for DynamDB

DynamoDB Hands On


DynamoDB Global Tables
    Make a DynamoDB table accessible with low latency in mulitple regions
    Active-Active replication (read/write to any AWS region)

Redshift Overview
    Redshift Overview
        Based on PostgresSQL but is not used for OLTP
        Its OLAP - online analytical processing (analytics and data warehousing)
        Load data once every hour not every second
        10x other data warehouse scale to PB of data
        Columnar storage of data  (instead of row based)
        Massively Parallel Query Execution MPP 
        Pay as you go 
        Has a SQL interface for perfoming queries
        BI tools such as AWS Quicksight or Tablueau integrate

    Redshift Serverless
        Automatically provision and scale data warehouse capacity
        Run analytics workloads without managing data warehouse infra
        Pay only for what you use
        Use case: reporting, dashboarding applications, real-time analytics

EMR OVerview 
    EMR stands for Elastic MapReduce
    EMR helps creating Hadoop clusters (Big Data) to analyze and process cast amount of data
    The clusters can be made of hundreds of EC2 instances
    Also supports Apache Spark, HBase, Presto , Flick
    EMR takes care of all the provisioning and configs
    Auto-scaling and integrated with spot insances
    Use cases: data processing machie learning web indexing big data

Athena Overview
    Serverless query service to perform analytics against S3 objects
    Uses standard SQL language to query the files
    Supports CVS, JSON, ORC, Avro, and Parquet 
    Pricing 5 per TB of data scanned
    Use compressed or columnar data for cost-savings
    Use case: Biz Intelligence / analytics/ reporting

    Exam: analyze data in S3 using serverless SQL  use Athena

QuickSight Overview
    Serverless machine learning powered business intelligence service to create interactive dashboards
    Fast automatically scalable embeddable with per-session pricing
    Intgrated with RDS, Aurora, Athena, Redshift, S3

DocumentDB Overview
    Aurora is an AWS implamentation of PostgresSQL / MySQL 
    DocumentDB is the same for MongoDB (which is a NoSQL database)
    MongoDB is used to store, querym and index JSON data
    Similar deployment concepts as Aurora
    Fully Managed HA with replication across 3 AZ
    DocumentDB storage automatically grows in increments of 10GB
    Automatically scales to workloads with millions of requests per seconds


Neptune Overview
    Fully managed graph database
    A popular graph dataset would be a social network
        Users have friends
        Posts have comments
        Comments have likes from users
        Users share adn like posts
    HA across 3 AZ with up to 15 read replicas
    Build and run applications working wiht highly connected datasets
    Can store upt to billions of relations and query the graph with ms latency
    HA with replications across 3 AZ
    Great for knowledge graphs , fraud detection, recommendation engins, social networking

Timestream Overview
    Fully managed fast scalable serverless time series database
    Auto scales up/down to adjust capacity
    Store and analyze trillions of events per day
    1000s times faster and 10th of cost of relational database
    Built-in time series analytics functions

Managed Blockchain Overview 
    Blockchain makes it possible to build applications where multiple parties can execute transactions without need for a trusted central authority
    Amazon managed blockchain is a managed service to 
        join public blockchain networks
        Create your own scalable private network
    Compatible with frameworks hyperledger fabric and ethereum

Glue Overview
    Managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service
    Useful to prepare and transform data for analytics
    Fully serverless service
    
    Glue is the middleman service to transform the data and then put into another DB

DMS Overview
    Database Migration Service
        Quickly and securely migrate database to AWS, resilient, self healing
        The source database remains available during migration

        [Source DB] ---> [EC2 Instance Running DMS] ---> [Target DB]

        Supports:
            Homogeneous migration
                Oracle to Oracle
            Heterogeneous migration
                Microsoft SQL Server to Aurora

Databases & Analytics Summary
    Relational Database - OTLP: RDS & Aurora (SQL)
    Differences between Multi-AZ, Read replicas, Multi-Region
    In-memory Database: ElastiCache
    Key/Valu Database: DynamoDB(serverless) & DAX (cache for DynamoDB)
    Warehouse - OLAP: Redshift (SQL)
    Hadoop Cluster: EMR big data analytics
    Athena: query data on Amazon S3 (serverless & SQL)
    QuickSight: dashboards / gui on your data (serverless)
    DocumentDB: Aurora for MongoDB (JSON NoSQL database)
    Amazon Managed Blockchain: managed Hyperledger fabrinc and Ethereum
    Glue: Managed ETL (Extract Transform Load) and Data Catalog
    Database Migration: DMS
    Neptune: graph database (social media)
    Timestream: time-series database
    